   doi       = {10.1016/j.ijhydene.2024.08.111},
   keywords  = {},
   addendum  = {19 September 2024 
"The well-known Chahine's rule states that there is a linear correlation between the H2 adsorption capacity and SSA, 1 wt\% excess H2 is adsorbed per 500 m2 g−1 increase in surface area at 77 K and medium pressure (3 bar), which is proposed to confirm that carbon materials with a high SSA will have high H2 adsorption performance [32]. However, García-Holley et al. pointed out that Chahine's rule applies to MOFs only when the SSA of MOFs is less than 3000 m2 g−1, and when the SSA of MOFs is greater than that value, the excess hydrogen storage is enhanced by 0.5 wt \% per 1000 m2 g−1 lift [33]. It can also be concluded from Fig. 3b that the increase of SSA can improve the hydrogen storage performance at low temperature (77 K) and high pressure.},
}

